Result:
```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1859,
    "summary": "A LockOperation whose userIndex is set but reads back as empty or untagged is now dropped. The credentialIndex walk used to run when the userIndex lookup missed, and that fallback is gone.",
    "failure_scenario": "A lock sends a keypad unlock with userIndex=0, a stale index, or an index whose GetUser returns null userStatus, while credentialIndex=7 belongs to the LCM-tagged user 'lcm:2:Alice'. On main, by_user_index returned None, the credential-index walk found slot 2, and the event fired. Now _raw_user_at returns None (or GetUser(0) fails and the walk finds no index 0), _lock_operation_slot returns None, and no code-slot event or credential_used fires. The PR says this can only happen if the lock contradicts itself, but nothing in the code or tests rules out such locks."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 516,
    "summary": "_raw_lock_user calls endpoint_of(node) (and later format_user) outside _invoke_sdk. A LockEndpointNotFoundError (a HomeAssistantError) is therefore never turned into a LockOperationError, so _raw_user_at's except doesn't catch it and skips the walk fallback. This read is now on every event.",
    "failure_scenario": "A node mid re-interview or with changed endpoints is resolved, and _get_lock_endpoint_or_raise raises LockEndpointNotFoundError while the argument for _invoke_sdk is being evaluated. The error escapes `except LockOperationError` in both _raw_user_at and _dispatch_lock_operation / _dispatch_lock_user_change, so the background task dies with an unhandled exception. On main, get_lock_users raised the same error inside _invoke_sdk, where it became LockDisconnected and was logged at debug."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1884,
    "summary": "The LockOperation path calls _raw_user_at (a GetUser, plus a full walk on failure) directly, not through coordinator.async_in_turn as LockUserChange does with the same helper. Its exchanges skip the worker that is supposed to own every exchange with the lock.",
    "failure_scenario": "The LCM entry unloads, or the worker stops or is backing off, while a keypad unlock event is queued. The LockOperation dispatch still sends GetUser, and the fallback may walk every user, to a lock that milestone 5c (1bd5f6ba) meant to be unreachable once the worker stops. It can also interleave with a write the worker has in flight. LockUserChange is protected against this; LockOperation, which now shares the read, is not."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 2037,
    "summary": "In the 'present but untagged' branch the anchor is dropped with _forget_user_index and its return value thrown away. A CLEAR that finds a foreign user already at that index can then no longer find the slot that was emptied.",
    "failure_scenario": "The homeowner deletes LCM's user at index 5 (anchored to slot 3) in the vendor app and adds their own user at index 5 before the follow-up GetUser arrives. The CLEAR event's read sees an untagged user, the first _forget_user_index pops slot 3 and discards it, and the second _forget_user_index for resolved.is_empty returns None. The event is ignored and slot 3 stays occupied with no correction, because push mode disables polling. The same happens on a lock that reports an empty user as userStatus=Available rather than null."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1841,
    "summary": "When the single read fails, _raw_user_at immediately walks every user. That is often the slowest possible response, and the walk is certain to fail when the cause is a missing client or node.",
    "failure_scenario": "The #1745 Aqara case, where a GetUser takes about 6 s while the lock re-locks: the single read times out, then 7 more GetUsers go to the same busy lock, so the event arrives later than it did on main. When _require_client_and_node raises LockDisconnected (no client or node), the walk raises the same error again, logging a misleading 'reading every user' message and doing extra work."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1345,
    "summary": "This is a root-cause gap the fix leaves open: _find_pin_credential_index_for_user still walks every user over the radio to find one user's PIN index, on every credential set or clear. That is the same one-user-by-full-walk pattern this PR fixes for events.",
    "failure_scenario": "On the 7-user Aqara U400, each LCM PIN write or clear still costs 7 GetUsers (about 2–4 s) before the actual command. Building _find_pin_credential_index_for_user on _raw_user_at(user_id) would remove that cost, and it would reuse the new helper instead of re-implementing the user_index match."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/providers/matter/conftest.py",
    "line": 279,
    "summary": "matter_helpers_patched answers the single-user read from the get_lock_users mock. Mocked-helper tests therefore can't tell a single read from a walk, and they never run the real _raw_lock_user (send_device_command plus _format_user_response).",
    "failure_scenario": "Suppose the event path goes back to walking every user, or _format_user_response stops returning creator_fabric_index (which short-tag resolution in _raw_user_slot needs). Every matter_helpers_patched test still passes, because the patched read builds its answer in the same shape as the test's get_lock_users input. Also, a test that queues side_effect failures on get_lock_users now has those entries used up by single reads, and `_asked(...) == ['get_lock_users']` (test_e2e.py:385) also counts single reads as walks. This breaks the 'tests must not build their own input contract' rule."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1948,
    "summary": "Several docstrings are now stale. _handle_lock_user_change still says the lookup is 'a fresh _raw_lock_users round-trip'. _raw_lock_user names only 'a use' as its event caller (LockUserChange also uses it) and says its LockDisconnected makes 'the caller fall back to the PIN-only release', which is false for the new _raw_user_at caller.",
    "failure_scenario": "A maintainer who reads _handle_lock_user_change or _raw_lock_user will think a LockUserChange walks every user, and that a failed single read leads to the PIN-only release. In fact it falls back to the walk. That is the kind of wrong model that led to this regression."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/matter.py",
    "line": 1854,
    "summary": "A new docstring uses the acronym 'PIN', which breaks AGENTS.md's code-style rule (AGENTS.md is the master guidance that CLAUDE.md points to).",
    "failure_scenario": "AGENTS.md Code Style says: \"**Spell out acronyms** in code comments instead of using abbreviations.\" The new line is: \"one, every user is read for the PIN at ``credentialIndex``.\""
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/providers/matter/test_provider.py",
    "line": 805,
    "summary": "Two test classes wrap the same _patch_user_reads helper in different ways: one uses `_patch_users = staticmethod(_patch_user_reads)` and the other a staticmethod def that just forwards to it.",
    "failure_scenario": "The two wrappers are duplicated indirection with no difference in behavior. Calling _patch_user_reads directly in both classes, or using the same alias form in each, would remove the duplication and the inconsistency."
  }
]
```

